This program demonstrates how to create and save a text file in C# using StreamWriter and SaveFileDialog.

Source Code
csharp
// Example of creating a new file

StreamWriter writer;
DialogResult result;

result = saveFileDialog1.ShowDialog();

if (result == DialogResult.OK) // Save
{
    // Dynamic
    writer = File.CreateText("c##.text");

    // writer = File.CreateText(saveFileDialog1.FileName);

    writer.WriteLine("shukri cabdullahi mohamud");
    writer.WriteLine("zada cabdullahi moxamud");

    writer.Close();
}
else if (result == DialogResult.Cancel)
{
    MessageBox.Show("not saved");
}

Explanation
StreamWriter writer;
Declares a StreamWriter variable used to write text into a file.
DialogResult result;
Declares a variable to store the result of the SaveFileDialog.
saveFileDialog1.ShowDialog();
Opens the Save File Dialog window.
if (result == DialogResult.OK)
Checks whether the user clicks the Save button.
File.CreateText("c##.text");
Creates a new text file named c##.text.
writer.WriteLine();
Writes text into the file. Each WriteLine creates a new line.
writer.Close();
Closes the file after writing.
else if (result == DialogResult.Cancel)
Checks whether the user cancels the save operation.
MessageBox.Show("not saved");
Displays a message when the user cancels.
Output

If the user clicks Save, the file contains:

text
shukri cabdullahi mohamud
zada cabdullahi moxamud


If the user clicks Cancel, a MessageBox displays:

not saved

Important Note

To save the file in the location selected by the user, use:

csharp
writer = File.CreateText(saveFileDialog1.FileName);


Instead of:

csharp
writer = File.CreateText("c##.text");


Also, use using System.IO; at the top of your C# file.

Conclusion
StreamWriter writes text into a file.
SaveFileDialog allows the user to choose where to save.
DialogResult.OK confirms saving.
DialogResult.Cancel cancels saving.
WriteLine() writes text into a new line.
Close() closes the file.


